1
00:02:48,750 --> 00:02:52,000
<i>Dr. Ross, we've got 
satellite from the Congo.</i>

2
00:02:53,460 --> 00:02:56,050
- What did he say, Rudy? 
- I can't repeat it here.

3
00:02:56,220 --> 00:02:58,430
- It's Charles. 
- It's Charles.

4
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:01,760
- It won't work. 
- He changed the code?

5
00:03:01,930 --> 00:03:04,270
The old man's got me changing it 
every three hours.

6
00:03:04,430 --> 00:03:09,020
- Rudy, Rudy, Rudy, Rudy. 
- Very inventive.

7
00:03:09,810 --> 00:03:12,110
Just in time. 
We should have a visual momentarily.

8
00:03:12,270 --> 00:03:14,740
- They said they found it. 
- You're kidding.

9
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:19,360
Seven volcanoes, and he picks the right 
one. His luck's finally changed.

10
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:24,370
- Karen. 
- I read you, Charles. And I see you.

11
00:03:24,540 --> 00:03:27,410
<i>Well, eureka and all that. 
I found the bloody thing.</i>

12
00:03:28,870 --> 00:03:31,420
- What was that? 
- The <i>usual.</i>

13
00:03:31,590 --> 00:03:33,500
<i>This whole place does the shimmy.</i>

14
00:03:33,670 --> 00:03:36,510
<i>- How's the volcano? 
- Acting very much like</i> a <i>volcano...</i>

15
00:03:36,670 --> 00:03:38,340
<i>thank you very much.</i>

16
00:03:38,470 --> 00:03:41,600
<i>They tell me it's fine, 
but I wouldn't build condominiums.</i>

17
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:43,970
Charles, did you really find it?

18
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:49,310
<i>Chemically flawless, 
blue diamond alluvia.</i>

19
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:54,270
<i>Johnny-on-the-spot phasic laser.</i>

20
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:57,780
<i>This will look kind of raw 
without a real diamond.</i>

21
00:04:08,410 --> 00:04:10,870
<i>Would you say that test 
came back positive?</i>

22
00:04:11,040 --> 00:04:13,380
Oh, my God.

23
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:18,220
<i>We found the sand in a mineral stream. 
Jeffrey's searching for the source.</i>

24
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:22,720
<i>- I'll get Travis. 
- Wait. Jeffrey should share the glory.</i>

25
00:04:22,890 --> 00:04:25,930
<i>- Let's link up again in, uh, one hour. 
- Great.</i>

26
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:28,270
- See you <i>in</i> an <i>hour.</i> 
- Give me your coordinates.

27
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:31,940
Char...? Charles...?

28
00:04:36,610 --> 00:04:39,990
- Jeffrey. 
- Charlie, over here.

29
00:04:42,450 --> 00:04:46,240
Charlie, I think this 
may be really something.

30
00:04:46,410 --> 00:04:49,080
- We haven't got much time. 
<i>- I'll</i> show you.

31
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:52,620
Jump in.

32
00:04:54,840 --> 00:04:57,000
- What? 
<i>- I'll</i> go first.

33
00:05:02,630 --> 00:05:04,140
I Jeffrey!

34
00:05:21,070 --> 00:05:22,400
I Jeffrey!

35
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:29,370
- What is this? 
- Come on.

36
00:05:30,750 --> 00:05:35,170
From below it just looks like jungle, 
but it's not just jungle. See?

37
00:05:35,330 --> 00:05:37,250
Jesus Christ.

38
00:05:38,170 --> 00:05:42,510
This is a big deal. 
This is a big find.

39
00:05:42,670 --> 00:05:48,680
I can't believe it's just sitting here. 
All these centuries, waiting for lucky us.

40
00:05:53,730 --> 00:05:57,560
Listen, I raised Houston, 
they're getting the old man.

41
00:05:58,150 --> 00:06:00,610
Come on camera and share the glory.

42
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:04,990
- We'll have to get another team here. 
- All right. Let me grab my bag.

43
00:06:16,830 --> 00:06:21,170
- Perfect, you say? A flawless diamond? 
- He found it. He was right.

44
00:06:21,340 --> 00:06:23,050
With the right laser and diamonds...

45
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:25,880
we can dominate 
the communications industry over night.

46
00:06:26,050 --> 00:06:27,180
Has he found the source?

47
00:06:27,390 --> 00:06:31,560
Charles says a laser with a diamond 
like that can punch a hole in the moon.

48
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:33,640
Shh. Don't say any more out here.

49
00:06:34,430 --> 00:06:35,980
- Where is he? 
- I don't know.

50
00:06:36,140 --> 00:06:39,150
- He should have transmitted 10 minutes ago. 
- Ugh.

51
00:06:39,310 --> 00:06:41,110
- Activate the remote. 
- Yes, sir.

52
00:06:47,950 --> 00:06:53,160
- I guess he's still looking for Jeffrey. 
- Hmm. Pan the camera.

53
00:07:00,750 --> 00:07:01,800
What in God's name...?

54
00:07:09,970 --> 00:07:13,430
Lock your remote. Back scan. 
Give me thermal readout.

55
00:07:17,730 --> 00:07:20,690
- I can see seven dead people. 
- What's that sound?

56
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:39,710
- I didn't see him. Charles! 
- What?

57
00:07:41,330 --> 00:07:44,210
<i>- I'll</i> get help. 
- You can't get out. I had the code changed.

58
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:46,590
Well, what is it?

59
00:07:47,130 --> 00:07:48,430
Rudy?

60
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:51,010
- Sir? 
- Use the fire extinguisher on that.

61
00:07:57,390 --> 00:08:02,020
- No one knows this happened but us. 
- Families have to be be notified. Your wife.

62
00:08:02,190 --> 00:08:06,190
We don't know he's dead. 
If it's reported, they'll close the border.

63
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:08,820
- What was that thing? 
- Uh, baboon.

64
00:08:08,990 --> 00:08:13,330
- I thought it was a gorilla, but I've never... 
- Gorillas didn't kill. Gorillas don't do that.

65
00:08:13,700 --> 00:08:17,620
- It could have been locals, a rival company. 
- Not another paranoid fantasy.

66
00:08:17,790 --> 00:08:20,080
I need those diamonds, Dr. Ross!

67
00:08:20,250 --> 00:08:22,540
- Are you for real? 
- It's the future.

68
00:08:22,710 --> 00:08:28,720
- Most believe children are the future. 
- 40,000 people, they all depend on me.

69
00:08:29,260 --> 00:08:31,720
This satellite is our cash cow.

70
00:08:31,890 --> 00:08:37,180
In three years it'll be obsolete. I need 
a new cash machine. This diamond's it!

71
00:08:37,350 --> 00:08:42,560
- Charles knew it. That's why he went. 
- He went into the Congo to please you.

72
00:08:42,730 --> 00:08:46,320
- I know you had feelings for him. 
- I almost married him.

73
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:48,530
Listen to me, talking like he's...

74
00:08:48,690 --> 00:08:50,400
You have to go down there, Dr. Ross.

75
00:08:50,570 --> 00:08:53,910
You're good in the field. You were 
good in Panama. Once you're local...

76
00:08:54,030 --> 00:08:57,040
that laser's power pack 
should give off a readable signal.

77
00:08:57,160 --> 00:09:00,330
Find some expedition that's already going. 
Take it over.

78
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:01,920
What are you talking about?

79
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:05,960
There's no time! Once we let out what's 
happened, Zaire will shut the border.

80
00:09:06,130 --> 00:09:08,420
- By the time the investigation's... 
- Are you human?

81
00:09:08,590 --> 00:09:12,680
I'll be human later. Hire people 
when you get there. Good people.

82
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:14,510
Never mind, I'll have some waiting.

83
00:09:14,890 --> 00:09:17,760
- Find out what happened to Charles. 
- Tell me you love your son.

84
00:09:17,930 --> 00:09:20,060
- I do. 
- And that's why you're sending me.

85
00:09:20,230 --> 00:09:22,140
It is.

86
00:09:22,310 --> 00:09:28,110
All right. But if I have one moment when 
I think otherwise, I'll make you sorry.

87
00:09:28,230 --> 00:09:29,820
That's a promise.

88
00:09:38,370 --> 00:09:40,580
<i>Oakland's number one classic station...</i>

89
00:09:40,750 --> 00:09:42,960
<i>taking you back in time, all the time.</i>

90
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:45,040
<i>There's 
a crazy little shack</i>

91
00:09:45,210 --> 00:09:51,050
Wow. Isn't that beautiful? 
What's Amy's favorite color?

92
00:09:51,210 --> 00:09:57,180
Green, that's right. 
How green was my valley... Drop that!

93
00:09:57,350 --> 00:10:01,430
Good. Pretty. So pretty.

94
00:10:01,560 --> 00:10:06,860
No, Amy, on the paper. Yeah, okay, good.

95
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:08,440
Amy, get some more paint.

96
00:10:11,070 --> 00:10:13,860
Good. That's green.

97
00:10:14,030 --> 00:10:17,820
No, Amy, on the paper. 
On... On the paper.

98
00:10:19,580 --> 00:10:23,040
Amy! Hey, you!

99
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:28,210
- How did she sleep? 
- Great night.

100
00:10:28,380 --> 00:10:30,170
- No nightmares? 
- Not a one.

101
00:10:30,340 --> 00:10:31,960
Good girl.

102
00:10:32,130 --> 00:10:33,970
That's my Amy.

103
00:10:35,010 --> 00:10:39,260
Richard, you better clean her up. 
It's showtime.

104
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:42,890
When we started out, it was so simple.

105
00:10:43,060 --> 00:10:47,440
Communication is what separated 
human from animal. Speech.

106
00:10:48,230 --> 00:10:51,690
And for those humans who had 
am impairment that inhibited speech...

107
00:10:51,860 --> 00:10:54,530
sign language. An alphabet in gestures.

108
00:10:54,690 --> 00:10:58,070
Words in motion. Then came virtual reality.

109
00:10:58,240 --> 00:11:03,950
Sensors worn like clothing could read 
and replicate the body's movements.

110
00:11:04,370 --> 00:11:10,840
Arliss Wender at MIT was the first to 
apply this technology to sign language.

111
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:15,880
<i>Our subject, William, 
was born without the organs of speech.</i>

112
00:11:16,050 --> 00:11:17,970
<i>Isn't that right, William?</i>

113
00:11:19,220 --> 00:11:23,180
<i>That is absolutely right.</i>

114
00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:34,190
<i>This is the first time 
I have ever heard my own voice.</i>

115
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:42,700
Lights, please. 
It's hard to get grants for what I do.

116
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:49,370
Most people just don't believe it. 
They think it's a parlor trick. A gag.

117
00:11:49,540 --> 00:11:51,670
But it's no gag, ladies and gentlemen.

118
00:11:51,840 --> 00:11:54,340
With this new technology, 
I can demonstrate it...

119
00:11:54,500 --> 00:11:58,590
in a way it has never been possible 
to demonstrate it before.

120
00:11:59,130 --> 00:12:00,800
Animals can talk.

121
00:12:02,050 --> 00:12:05,140
Amy, could you come down 
here please?

122
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:22,820
Hello, Amy. Thank you for the flowers.

123
00:12:22,990 --> 00:12:25,370
<i>Peter. Hello, Peter.</i>

124
00:12:25,540 --> 00:12:26,830
Wow.

125
00:12:28,250 --> 00:12:32,000
Amy, how old are you?

126
00:12:32,170 --> 00:12:37,590
<i>Amy 7. Amy good gorilla.</i>

127
00:12:38,210 --> 00:12:40,220
<i>- Amy pretty. 
</i>

128
00:12:41,220 --> 00:12:43,430
Amy, look at me.

129
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:48,980
- Amy mother. 
- That's right. That's your baby.

130
00:12:49,140 --> 00:12:53,440
<i>- Amy wants lunch.</i> 
- Okay.

131
00:12:54,110 --> 00:12:59,030
- What do you think, Mrs. Romy? 
- It's spooky. Very spooky.

132
00:12:59,190 --> 00:13:04,200
Amy's above average intelligence 
for a gorilla, but she's not a freak.

133
00:13:04,780 --> 00:13:08,540
What she has learned, 
other gorillas have learned and can learn.

134
00:13:08,700 --> 00:13:11,500
But that takes money, of course. 
Isn't that right, Amy?

135
00:13:30,770 --> 00:13:32,890
Amy, calm down. Easy.

136
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:36,360
Hey, hey, hey! Shh, shh. 
It's okay. You're all right.

137
00:13:38,320 --> 00:13:44,280
- You've got her sedated. She'll be fine. 
- She's not gonna be fine. Nightmares again.

138
00:13:44,410 --> 00:13:49,490
It's happened before. Gorillas in captivity 
degenerating mentally, having to be destroyed.

139
00:13:49,660 --> 00:13:53,040
When she did these paintings 
she got better. Why?

140
00:13:56,130 --> 00:14:01,840
Hey, hey... 
What's this room starting to look like?

141
00:14:04,010 --> 00:14:07,050
- The Jungle, she's been painting the jungle! 
- I don't know, Peter.

142
00:14:07,220 --> 00:14:08,350
She wants to go home.

143
00:14:08,510 --> 00:14:14,350
Peter, you did a great job. You taught a 
gorilla to talk, to paint, for gosh sakes.

144
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:18,440
When it comes to Amy, you can get a grant 
for anything except setting her free.

145
00:14:18,570 --> 00:14:21,230
How about this. I taught her, 
now she can teach them.

146
00:14:21,400 --> 00:14:22,990
- Who? 
- The mountain gorillas.

147
00:14:23,150 --> 00:14:26,280
We can translate 
what other animals are saying.

148
00:14:26,410 --> 00:14:29,530
Oh, please. Making you Dr. Doolittle?

149
00:14:29,700 --> 00:14:34,500
There isn't a foundation in motion that would 
fund an expedition with that objective.

150
00:14:34,660 --> 00:14:36,710
I will pay.

151
00:14:37,170 --> 00:14:41,460
- I will pay for Amy to go home. 
- And who are you?

152
00:14:42,170 --> 00:14:47,300
Herkermer Homolka. Formerly of Romania. 
Free now of the chains of Ceaucescu.

153
00:14:47,470 --> 00:14:50,680
Traveling the world, doing good.

154
00:14:53,890 --> 00:14:56,230
- A Romanian philanthropist? 
- Herkermer Homolka.

155
00:14:56,390 --> 00:14:59,190
- Something's voodoo. 
- Don't look a gift horse in the mouth.

156
00:14:59,360 --> 00:15:02,820
I don't think it's penetrating. 
We're taking Amy home. Get packed.

157
00:15:04,360 --> 00:15:08,740
Do you know where we're going? 
Amy, where are we going?

158
00:15:08,910 --> 00:15:12,540
- Jungle. <i>Me</i> jungle. 
- She's really excited.

159
00:15:12,700 --> 00:15:16,460
- Are you excited? 
- Me? I'm terrified.

160
00:15:16,620 --> 00:15:20,420
- Jungle. 
- Safari? I don't even like picnics. Nice glasses.

161
00:15:20,590 --> 00:15:22,710
<i>
Jungle. Me jungle.</i>

162
00:15:22,880 --> 00:15:27,300
<i>- That's right. Jungle. Home. 
- Tickle me. Tickle me.</i>

163
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:30,430
Come on, time to go.

164
00:15:40,770 --> 00:15:42,520
Someone looking for me?

165
00:15:44,150 --> 00:15:48,450
Herkermer Homolka, hello. 
Uh, the transport.

166
00:15:48,610 --> 00:15:50,030
Oh, yeah. Um...

167
00:15:50,200 --> 00:15:53,910
- ls there a problem? 
- Not unless there's a problem.

168
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:03,920
Are you excited, sweetheart? That's a 
camera. Be careful, it's expensive.

169
00:16:04,050 --> 00:16:07,050
I think she's got a real eye.

170
00:16:07,220 --> 00:16:11,930
- Karen Ross. Professor Karen Ross. 
- Professor Peter Elliot.

171
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:13,470
- Doctor. 
- Doctor.

172
00:16:13,640 --> 00:16:16,270
Have you got my faxes, 
Fed Exes and phone calls?

173
00:16:16,430 --> 00:16:18,560
- I'm sorry, you're the one. 
- Yes, I'm the one.

174
00:16:18,690 --> 00:16:20,730
We've put together a hasty expedition.

175
00:16:20,900 --> 00:16:23,060
To the Congo, with a talking gorilla.

176
00:16:23,230 --> 00:16:27,110
- Jungle me. Jungle. <i>Tickle</i> Amy. 
- That's right, jungle. Just a minute, Amy.

177
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:30,990
- I'm sorry... I'm afraid that's impossible. 
- I need to join up with you.

178
00:16:31,160 --> 00:16:32,620
- Excuse me. 
- Yeah?

179
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:35,950
- I have all the visas. 
- Go ahead and load this stuff on the plane.

180
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:38,620
- We're taking this stuff off the plane. 
- Off the plane?

181
00:16:38,790 --> 00:16:42,290
- I can pay part of the cost. 
- Why take stuff off the plane?

182
00:16:42,460 --> 00:16:46,590
- I'm sorry, we don't need any money. 
- You're flying way under capacity.

183
00:16:46,750 --> 00:16:48,300
You can easily find room for me.

184
00:16:48,460 --> 00:16:52,180
- It's not a matter of that. It's Amy. 
<i>- Ugly</i> woman.

185
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:54,550
- The gorilla? 
- She gets jealous very easily.

186
00:16:54,720 --> 00:16:57,060
Somebody tell me 
why you're unloading the plane.

187
00:16:57,220 --> 00:16:59,730
- Talk to the operations guy. 
- Can't you tell me?

188
00:16:59,890 --> 00:17:01,890
- No. 
- I've gotta get on this plane.

189
00:17:02,060 --> 00:17:06,110
I've got to be listed with your manifest, 
or my visas are worthless. Name your price.

190
00:17:06,270 --> 00:17:09,400
I don't have a price. I'm not a pound 
of sugar, I'm a primatologist.

191
00:17:09,570 --> 00:17:12,070
Don't be hasty, Dr. Elliot.

192
00:17:12,910 --> 00:17:15,660
Herkermer Homolka, 
formerly of Romania.

193
00:17:15,830 --> 00:17:18,620
- Dr. Karen Ross. 
- There seems to be some glitch.

194
00:17:18,790 --> 00:17:21,540
- They're unloading the plane. 
- Yes.

195
00:17:22,870 --> 00:17:27,300
Unfortunately there has been 
a slight interruption in my credit flow.

196
00:17:27,460 --> 00:17:31,090
- I am unable to pay for the fuel. 
- I'll pay for the fuel.

197
00:17:31,260 --> 00:17:35,680
<i>- I'll</i> pay for it. How much is the fuel? 
- Fifty-six thousand dollars.

198
00:17:35,800 --> 00:17:37,180
Fifty-six thousand?

199
00:17:38,770 --> 00:17:40,350
What are you smiling about?

200
00:17:40,520 --> 00:17:42,730
You need me, Dr. Elliot.

201
00:17:49,190 --> 00:17:51,860
<i>Taxi to</i> 
runway, three-one <i>left.</i>

202
00:17:52,780 --> 00:17:54,450
<i>This is your captain speaking.</i>

203
00:17:54,610 --> 00:17:56,660
<i>- Hey. 
- We've been cleared for take-off.</i>

204
00:17:56,820 --> 00:18:00,700
<i>Please make sure that your seat belts 
are fastened and that all carry-on...</i>

205
00:18:00,870 --> 00:18:04,460
We're returning her to the jungle 
from whence she came.

206
00:18:04,620 --> 00:18:06,630
From the look of it, not a moment too soon.

207
00:18:07,540 --> 00:18:11,630
That's it. Good girl. Here's an egg.

208
00:18:12,970 --> 00:18:13,970
Unh.

209
00:18:14,130 --> 00:18:17,300
We do not throw things. 
- ls that animal dangerous?

210
00:18:17,850 --> 00:18:20,470
Humans are dangerous, 
gorillas are very gentle.

211
00:18:25,850 --> 00:18:27,060
It's okay, Amy.

212
00:18:27,230 --> 00:18:29,650
<i>Where ground?</i>

213
00:18:29,820 --> 00:18:31,980
- Where ground? 
- It's fine. We're flying.

214
00:18:32,150 --> 00:18:37,280
- We're going to the jungle. 
- Amy want <i>green</i> drop <i>drink.</i>

215
00:18:37,450 --> 00:18:41,910
<i>- No. 
- Amy want green drop drink.</i>

216
00:18:42,080 --> 00:18:43,580
All right, all right.

217
00:18:45,040 --> 00:18:47,580
You'd swear they were married.

218
00:18:47,750 --> 00:18:53,710
The notion of the "killer ape" may be politically 
incorrect, but that's not to say it's untrue.

219
00:18:53,880 --> 00:18:55,590
I can't believe what I'm hearing.

220
00:18:57,340 --> 00:19:00,430
Cliches usually contain some 
element of truth.

221
00:19:06,140 --> 00:19:11,150
- Are you serving that ape a martini? 
- She's allowed one. It'll calm her down.

222
00:19:13,070 --> 00:19:16,820
In my life I have heard 
many a bizarre story.

223
00:19:16,990 --> 00:19:22,580
Many a bizarre story 
containing bizarre truth.

224
00:19:47,690 --> 00:19:51,480
- There you go. Watch your step. 
- Dr. Ross!

225
00:19:53,320 --> 00:19:56,360
- Dr. Ross! 
- Hello?

226
00:19:56,530 --> 00:20:02,200
Eddie Ventro. Mr Travis asked me 
to meet your plane, set up your expedition.

227
00:20:02,370 --> 00:20:06,540
- Her expedition? She's just along for the ride. 
- Whatever. Be an asshole.

228
00:20:06,700 --> 00:20:11,330
- Who is this guy? 
- Eddie Ventro, transportation and equipment.

229
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:15,960
But I don't supply assholes with new 
personalities. Wanna see your new plane?

230
00:20:16,130 --> 00:20:18,420
Let's go! Hold on.

231
00:20:21,760 --> 00:20:23,760
- Wanna sell that gorilla? 
- No.

232
00:20:23,930 --> 00:20:25,470
- Worth a lot of money. 
- How much?

233
00:20:25,640 --> 00:20:28,100
- Mr. Homolka, I can't believe you. 
- I'm just curious.

234
00:20:28,270 --> 00:20:30,480
Twenty, twenty-five grand for a female.

235
00:20:31,310 --> 00:20:36,190
- Amy want to go home. Go home. 
- I'm taking you home,</i> Amy.

236
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:42,110
Whoa, a talking gorilla! I feel the money hairs 
on the back of my neck going woo, woo, woo.

237
00:20:42,620 --> 00:20:46,910
Mr. Travis told me to load you up. The 
works. You know how to use all this stuff?

238
00:20:47,080 --> 00:20:49,290
- I'll get by. 
- You really need a balloon?

239
00:20:49,460 --> 00:20:51,120
No, I don't. Let me see that.

240
00:20:52,830 --> 00:20:55,210
- Who are they? 
- Hi, fellas!

241
00:20:55,840 --> 00:20:59,590
Fuck if I know. Don't ask. 
We're on our third government in two years.

242
00:20:59,760 --> 00:21:02,550
- So are you our guide? 
- No, I stay at the airport.

243
00:21:02,720 --> 00:21:04,180
Supposing you got Monroe Kelly.

244
00:21:04,350 --> 00:21:07,680
- Is he good? 
- He's very good. He's also very late.

245
00:21:08,100 --> 00:21:09,390
Whoa.

246
00:21:09,560 --> 00:21:11,850
- Holy shit, now what? 
- What was that?

247
00:21:11,980 --> 00:21:15,020
Haven't got a clue. I feel like 
this airport's just gone to shit.

248
00:21:24,370 --> 00:21:28,790
- All right, give me your passports. 
- We already arranged for a guide.

249
00:21:28,950 --> 00:21:30,830
- Robertson Reynolds. 
- Yeah, I fired him.

250
00:21:30,100 --> 00:21:32,790
- You what? 
- Reynolds is a bird-watcher.

251
00:21:32,960 --> 00:21:35,290
- I hired Robertson Reynolds. 
- You would.

252
00:21:35,460 --> 00:21:38,960
You have any idea what's going on 
in the Congo as of this morning?

253
00:21:39,130 --> 00:21:42,680
The Kigani have had it with Zaire 
and they're eating people.

254
00:21:42,840 --> 00:21:46,260
You go in with Reynolds, you're coming out 
somebody's bowel movement.

255
00:21:46,430 --> 00:21:49,600
There you go. Now wasn't that 
easier than going through customs?

256
00:21:49,810 --> 00:21:51,810
Monroe.

257
00:21:52,770 --> 00:21:54,770
- Guess what? 
- What?

258
00:21:54,900 --> 00:21:57,190
- We need to switch airports. 
- Oh, shit, why?

259
00:21:57,360 --> 00:21:58,820
Bomb in the president's car.

260
00:21:58,980 --> 00:22:01,320
It was the president's car? 
Did they get him?

261
00:22:01,490 --> 00:22:04,200
That's the bad news. No, they didn't.

262
00:22:04,360 --> 00:22:06,950
- I hope you folks have lots of money. 
- We do.

263
00:22:15,960 --> 00:22:19,590
Thank you. Now run away. 
Oh, come on, man, run away.

264
00:22:20,340 --> 00:22:22,470
- Get in the back. 
- What about equipment?

265
00:22:22,630 --> 00:22:24,720
Leave it to Eddie. I would hurry, really.

266
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:26,550
- Is this normal? 
- Get in the truck.

267
00:22:26,720 --> 00:22:29,060
- You're on your own, Eddie. 
- Yeah, how I like it.

268
00:22:31,560 --> 00:22:34,270
- Where do you want me to set you up? 
- Get a DC-3.

269
00:22:34,440 --> 00:22:38,020
A grass strip south of Kegoma. Get it 
stripped. Keep this from the company.

270
00:22:38,190 --> 00:22:42,900
First the Kigani in Zaire, now this godfather 
stuff. Not a good day to be in Africa.

271
00:22:43,070 --> 00:22:45,450
- Lower the flaps. 
- So long, good luck!

272
00:22:45,610 --> 00:22:47,450
Now sit down.

273
00:23:08,550 --> 00:23:11,100
I'm getting out of here. Unh!

274
00:23:20,480 --> 00:23:23,780
- What's going on? 
- Are we safe?

275
00:23:25,360 --> 00:23:27,990
It's all right, just bumpy. 
Just a few bumps.

276
00:23:33,120 --> 00:23:35,160
Amy, leave that man alone.

277
00:23:41,090 --> 00:23:43,130
Amy, don't inhale.

278
00:23:45,170 --> 00:23:47,260
- Karen Ross? 
- That's me.

279
00:23:48,260 --> 00:23:51,220
Monroe Kelly. 
I'm your great white hunter for this trip.

280
00:23:51,390 --> 00:23:56,230
- Though I happen to be black. 
- How bad is this news for us?

281
00:23:56,390 --> 00:24:01,110
Whenever the leadership of one of these little 
Central African countries comes into question...

282
00:24:01,270 --> 00:24:02,940
they tend to murder everybody.

283
00:24:03,110 --> 00:24:05,110
- Oh, my God! 
- Not really?

284
00:24:05,280 --> 00:24:07,570
They live for the opportunity 
to settle scores.

285
00:24:07,740 --> 00:24:12,240
And they've got a lot of scores to settle. 
It's like what's going on in the Congo.

286
00:24:12,410 --> 00:24:15,700
- Things are pretty bad in the Congo. 
- We heard.

287
00:24:15,870 --> 00:24:18,580
The Kigani are pissed off.

288
00:24:18,750 --> 00:24:24,000
Can't blame them. The 20th century sucks. 
Maybe the 21st will be better.

289
00:24:24,170 --> 00:24:26,960
- You some kind of criminal, aren't you? 
- Aren't we all?

290
00:24:27,130 --> 00:24:29,720
No. I'm not a criminal, I'm a scientist.

291
00:24:29,880 --> 00:24:31,590
Scientist?

292
00:24:31,760 --> 00:24:35,970
I run a few guns, 
you sons of bitches ruin the world.

293
00:24:36,520 --> 00:24:40,390
I don't think it's a good idea to have 
further involvement with these people.

294
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:42,770
What would you suggest we do, Dr. Elliot?

295
00:24:43,400 --> 00:24:46,480
As soon as we can, 
we should go our own way.

296
00:24:47,480 --> 00:24:49,900
- Ugh. 
- Don't I know you?

297
00:24:51,740 --> 00:24:54,740
Perhaps. I travel a great deal.

298
00:24:55,450 --> 00:24:58,330
- What are you doing with that gorilla? 
- Taking her home.

299
00:24:58,450 --> 00:25:00,960
She was caught 
in the Virunga region of the Congo.

300
00:25:01,120 --> 00:25:04,880
You're going to try and take her back 
to the farm now that she's seen Paris?

301
00:25:05,710 --> 00:25:10,170
- And this is your cover? 
- Yes.

302
00:25:10,630 --> 00:25:12,930
Pretty good. Or, it would have been.

303
00:25:13,090 --> 00:25:17,350
- I'm nobody's cover. Cover for what? 
- You find yourself in the middle of something.

304
00:25:17,510 --> 00:25:20,390
- Cover for what? 
- I don't know, and she won't tell you.

305
00:25:20,560 --> 00:25:23,150
But the kind of money 
her company is throwing around?

306
00:25:23,310 --> 00:25:26,110
They don't spend that for any gorilla.

307
00:25:27,610 --> 00:25:31,950
Relax. You're in better hands 
than you should be.

308
00:26:14,110 --> 00:26:17,780
- Monroe. 
- Captain Wanta.

309
00:26:17,950 --> 00:26:23,120
How are you? 
Miss Ross, such a pleasure.

310
00:26:25,830 --> 00:26:28,960
Have some, uh, coffee and cake.

311
00:26:33,340 --> 00:26:37,260
You have our visas. Our manifest. 
This is a legitimate scientific mission.

312
00:26:37,430 --> 00:26:39,390
You have no right to detain us.

313
00:26:39,510 --> 00:26:42,850
Liar, liar, pants on fire.

314
00:26:43,810 --> 00:26:47,100
So says my computer, Miss Ross.

315
00:26:50,900 --> 00:26:55,360
Ah. You used to work for the CIA. 
And now you're TraviCom.

316
00:26:55,530 --> 00:26:59,370
- TraviCom pays better than the CIA? 
- You have a big mouth.

317
00:26:59,530 --> 00:27:03,450
Everybody says that about me. 
What can I do?

318
00:27:04,620 --> 00:27:07,120
Monroe, you have 
the worst timing in the world.

319
00:27:07,290 --> 00:27:08,420
Tell me about it.

320
00:27:08,580 --> 00:27:11,090
They blew up the president's Mercedes, 
did you know?

321
00:27:11,250 --> 00:27:15,630
It's a big plot. Our country will 
be in shit for at least a year.

322
00:27:15,800 --> 00:27:18,050
- Captain, I need that truck. 
- You need the truck.

323
00:27:18,220 --> 00:27:21,220
You also need ten good men to the border.

324
00:27:30,110 --> 00:27:32,440
More.

325
00:27:45,790 --> 00:27:48,500
Mr. Homolka?

326
00:27:49,830 --> 00:27:51,460
Mm.

327
00:27:51,630 --> 00:27:53,170
Stop eating my sesame cake.

328
00:27:54,880 --> 00:27:56,340
Mm.

329
00:27:57,970 --> 00:28:01,600
What are you doing in my country, 
you bag of shit?

330
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:06,310
Captain, please. 
I only wish to explore and discover.

331
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:12,610
This fellow is a big bag of shit.

332
00:28:12,770 --> 00:28:18,650
Shake this rat from off your neck. He owes 
money to everybody everywhere he goes.

333
00:28:18,820 --> 00:28:21,200
I will ask you to wait outside, Mr. 
Homolka.

334
00:28:26,580 --> 00:28:30,670
You know, it's the gorilla that's going 
to get you across the border safely.

335
00:28:31,210 --> 00:28:36,460
Everybody's so afraid of being seen in an 
American movie being cruel to a gorilla.

336
00:28:37,050 --> 00:28:41,640
This is the crazy world we live in. 
Okay, you can go.

337
00:28:43,800 --> 00:28:47,560
Hit the road, as they say. 
And have a nice day.

338
00:29:04,950 --> 00:29:06,700
- You all right? 
- Yeah.

339
00:29:06,870 --> 00:29:08,100
Admit it, you're glad to see me.

340
00:29:57,960 --> 00:30:00,420
- Kahega! 
- Ah, Monroe!

341
00:30:03,340 --> 00:30:05,340
Kikuyu tribesman, very nice guy.

342
00:30:05,470 --> 00:30:08,140
- Are we in Zaire? 
- Tanzania.

343
00:30:26,620 --> 00:30:31,160
Stay close to me. Amy. Amy, come on.

344
00:30:41,380 --> 00:30:45,090
- Somehow, I think this is at my expense. 
- You're right.

345
00:30:50,100 --> 00:30:52,890
When do you think we will 
get to the rainforest?

346
00:30:53,060 --> 00:30:56,100
- Herkermer Homolka, right? 
- Yes. Hi, hello.

347
00:30:56,270 --> 00:30:59,060
You're not looking for 
Solomon's city again, are you?

348
00:30:59,190 --> 00:31:01,150
What are you talking about?

349
00:31:01,320 --> 00:31:03,900
Mr. Homolka was part of 
a disastrous expedition...

350
00:31:04,030 --> 00:31:06,660
- about, what, five years ago? 
- I would prefer you...

351
00:31:06,820 --> 00:31:10,530
They were looking for a city called Zinj. 
Unfortunately, it doesn't exist.

352
00:31:10,700 --> 00:31:12,790
- It does exist! 
- Fine.

353
00:31:12,910 --> 00:31:15,750
Three members of his safari 
died from exposure.

354
00:31:15,910 --> 00:31:20,250
- A fourth was shot by we don't know who. 
- It was an appalling suicide.

355
00:31:20,420 --> 00:31:24,210
We won't quibble. And Mr. Homolka 
had to be carried out of the jungle...

356
00:31:24,380 --> 00:31:25,970
by, as I remember it, me.

357
00:31:26,130 --> 00:31:28,430
I knew he wasn't a Romanian philanthropist.

358
00:31:28,590 --> 00:31:33,310
Oh, he's Romanian. And he has 
done good, but mostly for Mr. Homolka.

359
00:31:40,310 --> 00:31:43,440
- What's all this stuff? 
- Courtesy of Eddie Ventro.

360
00:31:43,610 --> 00:31:44,940
Eddie! What happened?

361
00:31:45,110 --> 00:31:50,030
Ugh. A customs guy hit me with a can 
of peanut oil for stamping visas.

362
00:31:50,200 --> 00:31:52,160
I'm never going back to that country, man.

363
00:31:52,330 --> 00:31:54,450
- Those people have permanently wigged out. 
- Ha, ha.

364
00:31:54,620 --> 00:31:56,120
- Dr. Ross. 
- Hi.

365
00:31:56,290 --> 00:32:01,420
Equipment list. You got everything but the 
balloon and the home entertainment center.

366
00:32:01,540 --> 00:32:04,500
- Ah. Your boss says "Call me." 
- Okay.

367
00:32:04,670 --> 00:32:08,590
You got 11 good guys, significant 
firepower, significant technology.

368
00:32:08,760 --> 00:32:11,260
More than that 
I don't think you're gonna need. Agree?

369
00:32:11,430 --> 00:32:12,720
- I agree. 
- Good luck.

370
00:32:12,890 --> 00:32:15,020
- Bye, Eddie. 
- Bye, Monroe.

371
00:32:39,330 --> 00:32:43,130
- May I? 
- There's one thing I don't understand, Monroe.

372
00:32:43,290 --> 00:32:44,500
What's that, Dr. Elliot?

373
00:32:44,670 --> 00:32:46,380
If the border to Zaire is shut...

374
00:32:46,550 --> 00:32:49,380
- how do we get across? 
- Luck.

375
00:33:11,820 --> 00:33:13,030
What's going on?

376
00:33:13,700 --> 00:33:15,530
What the hell is that?

377
00:33:16,870 --> 00:33:21,960
Shit. I thought we'd get farther. 
I'd put her out now.

378
00:33:22,120 --> 00:33:24,790
- Oh, you would? Who's firing on us? 
- Kahega!

379
00:33:24,960 --> 00:33:27,750
We crossed the border into Zaire. 
We're violating airspace.

380
00:33:27,920 --> 00:33:29,420
- She likes bananas? 
- Sure.

381
00:33:29,590 --> 00:33:32,760
Here, eat this, darling. 
She's going to feel great.

382
00:33:32,930 --> 00:33:36,970
- What do you mean? 
- Something to keep her calm. Gorilla Prozac.

383
00:33:38,850 --> 00:33:40,680
- Can we make it to Bondo? 
- I don't know!

384
00:33:40,850 --> 00:33:43,310
That last one was a heat-seeker. 
We're done.

385
00:33:56,450 --> 00:33:59,330
- What are you doing? 
- Step aside, please.

386
00:34:21,060 --> 00:34:22,890
<i>Hey-</i>

387
00:34:46,290 --> 00:34:48,380
Miss Ross. Here.

388
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:53,880
- Why are they putting on parachutes? 
- Figure it out.

389
00:34:54,050 --> 00:34:56,180
- Take it. 
- Oh, no!

390
00:35:09,190 --> 00:35:12,730
- This isn't gonna work. 
- Yes it is. I'll take Amy.

391
00:35:13,780 --> 00:35:16,990
- She might get hurt. I can't risk it. 
- You know how to fly this plane?

392
00:35:17,160 --> 00:35:19,240
- No. 
- The pilot and co-pilot are gone.

393
00:35:19,410 --> 00:35:21,280
So, what are you going to do?

394
00:35:22,910 --> 00:35:24,750
Ugh.

395
00:35:25,210 --> 00:35:26,960
Go! Go, go, go!

396
00:35:38,300 --> 00:35:39,850
Push me, please.

397
00:35:42,100 --> 00:35:43,930
Harder, please.

398
00:36:05,370 --> 00:36:07,710
You want me to push you too?

399
00:36:11,790 --> 00:36:14,250
See you down there.

400
00:36:16,300 --> 00:36:18,380
Oh!

401
00:37:22,280 --> 00:37:24,780
- Where's Amy? 
- Now she's asleep.

402
00:37:24,950 --> 00:37:28,080
Over there. On the way down, 
she decided to wake up.

403
00:37:28,240 --> 00:37:32,960
- And that little lady has some set of teeth. 
- I'm sorry, but we have to call this off.

404
00:37:33,120 --> 00:37:34,960
- I beg your pardon? 
- I'm in over my head.

405
00:37:35,130 --> 00:37:37,800
- I'm not James Bond. 
- Don't worry, I'll take care of you.

406
00:37:37,960 --> 00:37:40,260
I don't want you to take care of me.

407
00:37:40,970 --> 00:37:45,050
Fine. I'm taking a safari 
into the Virunga region of the Congo.

408
00:37:45,180 --> 00:37:48,430
If you don't want to go, just go wherever.

409
00:37:48,600 --> 00:37:53,230
But you should know this is a dangerous 
place, and people die here very easily.

410
00:37:54,100 --> 00:37:56,230
Now, excuse me.

411
00:38:04,530 --> 00:38:05,780
Amy?

412
00:38:10,700 --> 00:38:12,960
Amy? Amy!

413
00:38:14,040 --> 00:38:15,880
Amy?

414
00:38:23,260 --> 00:38:26,800
Such an extraordinary animal.

415
00:38:26,970 --> 00:38:31,730
Those paintings. Do you think she was 
drawing something she saw in life?

416
00:38:31,890 --> 00:38:33,230
Or in a dream, maybe?

417
00:38:33,390 --> 00:38:37,610
- I think she was drawing a jungle, home. 
- What about those oval shapes?

418
00:38:37,770 --> 00:38:41,780
- Just a shape she likes, something like that. 
- Perhaps.

419
00:38:42,610 --> 00:38:45,610
- Video game? 
- It gives latitude and longitude.

420
00:38:45,740 --> 00:38:50,790
- More than that, from the look of it. 
- Well, you know, it's a gadget. It has features.

421
00:38:50,950 --> 00:38:53,660
You're picking up a signal. 
Someone or something?

422
00:38:53,830 --> 00:38:55,750
- A little bit Of both. 
- Ah.

423
00:38:55,920 --> 00:39:00,090
- So why did you quit the CIA? 
- I never worked for the CIA.

424
00:39:00,250 --> 00:39:01,420
Of course you didn't.

425
00:39:01,590 --> 00:39:04,470
But if you had worked for the CIA, 
why would you quit?

426
00:39:04,630 --> 00:39:07,050
Because they're a loveless bunch 
of sons of bitches.

427
00:39:07,180 --> 00:39:10,010
- And you're not? 
- And I'm not.

428
00:39:10,140 --> 00:39:11,970
Glad to hear it.

429
00:39:18,900 --> 00:39:23,530
- Okay, here we go. 
- Ah. Fabulous equipment.

430
00:39:23,690 --> 00:39:26,110
- Best of the best. 
- I have air-conditioning, too.

431
00:39:26,280 --> 00:39:28,240
Air-conditioners?

432
00:39:28,410 --> 00:39:32,200
- I suppose it is a bit much. 
- Shit, I'll take one.

433
00:39:36,370 --> 00:39:37,620
Hey, Stop it!

434
00:39:42,750 --> 00:39:44,800
Mr. Homolka...

435
00:39:44,960 --> 00:39:50,640
- Please, call me Herkermer. 
- All right. You can call me Mr. Kelly.

436
00:39:52,680 --> 00:39:56,980
- I wonder what she's about. 
- What do you mean?

437
00:39:57,140 --> 00:39:59,440
She's after something. 
You're after something.

438
00:39:59,600 --> 00:40:03,440
What you're after, I've got figured. 
But what she's after, I don't know.

439
00:40:03,610 --> 00:40:05,650
I'll get you! I'll get you!

440
00:40:06,070 --> 00:40:11,570
I'm helping that young scientist 
return his ward to the wild.

441
00:40:11,700 --> 00:40:15,950
He says the ape may be able to tell us 
the thoughts of the wild gorillas.

442
00:40:16,120 --> 00:40:17,830
Well I can tell you your thoughts.

443
00:40:17,100 --> 00:40:21,580
You're looking for King Solomon's 
diamonds. The lost city of Zinj.

444
00:40:24,460 --> 00:40:26,760
- I've given it up. 
- You'll never give it up.

445
00:40:26,920 --> 00:40:29,760
The problem is, Herkermer, 
it doesn't exist.

446
00:40:29,930 --> 00:40:34,890
Solomon must have gotten his diamonds 
from Cartiers, like everybody else.

447
00:40:39,560 --> 00:40:43,730
Ha-ha-ha. You like that, don't you? 
Ha-ha-ha.

448
00:40:45,650 --> 00:40:48,570
Time for Madam Amy's vitamins.

449
00:40:55,080 --> 00:40:58,120
This is Karen Ross. 81452.

450
00:40:58,290 --> 00:41:03,590
That is KR81452. 
Houston, Travis, do you read?

451
00:41:04,340 --> 00:41:09,050
<i>Karen Ross, 81452. 
Houston, Travis, do you read?</i>

452
00:41:09,220 --> 00:41:14,100
I read and see you, Dr. Ross. 
Congratulations on crossing the border.

453
00:41:15,470 --> 00:41:18,060
<i>I'm picking up a signal 
from the laser's power pack.</i>

454
00:41:18,180 --> 00:41:20,940
Excellent. Wherever the laser is, 
Charles should be too.

455
00:41:21,100 --> 00:41:25,520
Let's hope so. Tomorrow we enter the 
rainforest at the base of the volcanic range.

456
00:41:25,690 --> 00:41:30,780
<i>Good. Karen, I've had those images 
of that creature analyzed. Rudy.</i>

457
00:41:37,240 --> 00:41:40,330
<i>What's W? 
- My primatologist tells me it's something new.</i>

458
00:41:40,500 --> 00:41:42,710
<i>He doesn't know what, 
but it's gorilla-like.</i>

459
00:41:42,870 --> 00:41:44,750
Are you sufficiently armed?

460
00:41:44,920 --> 00:41:47,710
- Do you have enough manpower? 
<i>- Yes.</i>

461
00:41:47,880 --> 00:41:51,590
Good. The bad news is Geo-thermal. 
You've got to hurry.

462
00:41:51,760 --> 00:41:55,640
That region in the Virungas is bad. 
Sometimes the volcanic activity...

463
00:41:55,800 --> 00:41:58,770
<i>is breaking out of the graph. 
The region's going to blow.</i>

464
00:41:58,930 --> 00:42:01,520
<i>Could be a week, could be sooner. 
Dr. Ross...</i>

465
00:42:01,680 --> 00:42:03,900
- What the hell...? 
- Watch out!

466
00:42:04,060 --> 00:42:07,520
No, dammit! Not again!

467
00:42:07,860 --> 00:42:10,190
Oh. I'm terribly sorry.

468
00:42:13,740 --> 00:42:15,370
It's destroyed.

469
00:42:17,200 --> 00:42:19,910
It's completely destroyed.

470
00:43:28,100 --> 00:43:31,940
It's Amy's medical supplies. 
They can't get wet.

471
00:43:55,670 --> 00:43:59,510
Beautiful boats. 
Money's a wonderful thing.

472
00:43:59,680 --> 00:44:01,600
Give her the banana with the dope inside?

473
00:44:01,760 --> 00:44:05,220
Yes, he gave her the banana 
with the dope inside.

474
00:44:05,390 --> 00:44:08,600
I know. Shitty Peter. I know.

475
00:44:08,770 --> 00:44:11,060
Hey, what's this?

476
00:44:12,860 --> 00:44:14,400
Oh.

477
00:44:14,980 --> 00:44:17,360
All <i>the</i> leaves are brown

478
00:44:17,530 --> 00:44:20,280
<i>And the sky is gray</i>

479
00:44:20,450 --> 00:44:22,910
<i>I've been for a walk</i>

480
00:44:23,080 --> 00:44:24,990
<i>On</i> a <i>winters day</i>

481
00:44:25,160 --> 00:44:26,910
<i>On a winters day</i>

482
00:44:27,080 --> 00:44:30,630
<i>I'd be safe and warm</i>

483
00:44:30,790 --> 00:44:34,710
<i>If I was in L.A.</i>

484
00:44:34,840 --> 00:44:38,550
<i>- California dreaming 
- California dreaming</i>

485
00:44:38,720 --> 00:44:42,470
<i>On such a winters day</i>

486
00:44:42,640 --> 00:44:46,970
<i>Stopped into a church</i>

487
00:44:57,610 --> 00:44:59,860
Watch out for the rock!

488
00:45:01,110 --> 00:45:02,240
Whoa!

489
00:45:03,780 --> 00:45:05,330
Hang on!

490
00:45:24,010 --> 00:45:29,230
- What is this Zinj, Mr. Homolka? 
- I don't know what you're talking about.

491
00:45:29,390 --> 00:45:32,770
It's been believed that King Solomon 
had a diamond mine in the Congo...

492
00:45:32,940 --> 00:45:34,400
in a city called Zinj.

493
00:45:34,810 --> 00:45:36,520
Diamonds?

494
00:45:39,610 --> 00:45:43,570
I suppose there's no point 
in playing stupid.

495
00:45:43,740 --> 00:45:48,660
We shall arrive there together, 
we shall all be rich.

496
00:45:48,830 --> 00:45:51,620
That's fine with me. 
Except there's no Zinj.

497
00:45:51,750 --> 00:45:54,960
Half the safaris that have gone in looking 
never made it back.

498
00:45:55,130 --> 00:45:56,920
The other half came back with nothing.

499
00:45:57,090 --> 00:46:01,760
Yes, but we have a trumping card 
that they did not have.

500
00:46:01,920 --> 00:46:05,680
- We do? 
- We do. That gorilla...

501
00:46:06,220 --> 00:46:08,350
- knows where it is. 
- What?

502
00:46:08,470 --> 00:46:10,180
The gorilla?

503
00:46:10,310 --> 00:46:14,600
As a young man, 
I found a book in Soviet Georgia.

504
00:46:14,770 --> 00:46:19,230
It contained a detailed drawing 
of the city of Zinj.

505
00:46:19,780 --> 00:46:23,280
The drawing contained 
a peculiar decoration.

506
00:46:23,860 --> 00:46:26,240
An open eye.

507
00:46:26,410 --> 00:46:30,100
Later, on safari, I found this.

508
00:46:34,330 --> 00:46:36,920
It's the same symbol. Now...

509
00:46:38,590 --> 00:46:40,300
look at this.

510
00:46:42,800 --> 00:46:45,050
The gorilla drew this.

511
00:46:47,430 --> 00:46:52,310
It is the same image. It is the open eye.

512
00:46:53,020 --> 00:46:58,020
That gorilla has seen the city of Zinj.

513
00:47:01,190 --> 00:47:05,910
- And she will take us there! 
- Have you been taking your medication?

514
00:47:09,910 --> 00:47:13,160
Have your laughing, and I will have mine.

515
00:47:20,630 --> 00:47:21,880
Shit...

516
00:47:23,550 --> 00:47:26,720
- What is it? 
- The signal stopped.

517
00:47:26,880 --> 00:47:31,390
- Does that mean...? 
- It just means the signal stopped.

518
00:47:32,600 --> 00:47:34,270
I don't know what it means.

519
00:48:34,490 --> 00:48:36,160
No problem.

520
00:49:03,060 --> 00:49:04,190
Oh, my God!

521
00:49:14,660 --> 00:49:16,790
- Shoot it! 
- Amy!

522
00:49:16,950 --> 00:49:20,460
- What is it? 
- Turn the boat around, quickly.

523
00:49:23,790 --> 00:49:26,090
Agh! Aah!

524
00:49:26,800 --> 00:49:29,300
Shoot it! Oh, my God!

525
00:49:31,090 --> 00:49:33,010
Turn the boat around!

526
00:49:37,180 --> 00:49:39,060
Head for shore!

527
00:49:55,570 --> 00:49:57,410
- How is he? 
- It's just a big cut.

528
00:49:57,580 --> 00:49:59,790
- I'll stitch it up, he'll be all right. 
- Oh, God.

529
00:50:01,330 --> 00:50:04,130
- What's that? 
- A plane.

530
00:50:05,080 --> 00:50:07,460
- Good luck to them. 
- Who are they?

531
00:50:07,630 --> 00:50:11,760
I don't know, but the Zaire Air Force 
has got a piece of their ass.

532
00:50:16,180 --> 00:50:19,430
Pitch camp. Tomorrow, Mt. Mukenko.

533
00:50:39,080 --> 00:50:41,580
- She's getting excited. 
- Why, do you think?

534
00:50:41,750 --> 00:50:45,420
Somewhere on the other side of this 
mountain is where Amy was born.

535
00:51:13,440 --> 00:51:15,650
Wow, it's beautiful.

536
00:51:17,450 --> 00:51:20,450
Is this your valley, Amy? 
It is, isn't it?

537
00:51:20,620 --> 00:51:23,660
It's home. That's your home.

538
00:51:25,870 --> 00:51:30,000
Pretty jungle, that's right.

539
00:51:35,630 --> 00:51:37,130
What's she doing?

540
00:51:37,300 --> 00:51:40,890
She wants to go to the place in her heart.

541
00:51:41,060 --> 00:51:46,810
- You mean the place in your heart, Herkermer. 
- You may call me Mr. Homolka.

542
00:51:48,310 --> 00:51:50,650
Come on, Amy, let's go.

543
00:51:52,730 --> 00:51:54,610
- We've lost two porters. 
- Where'd they go?

544
00:51:54,780 --> 00:51:58,200
- Ran away. 
- And they didn't invite me?

545
00:51:58,360 --> 00:52:01,950
You want your glove? All right, sit still.

546
00:52:05,540 --> 00:52:12,040
This, as they say, is a fork in the road. 
We could go left or we could go right.

547
00:52:12,210 --> 00:52:14,380
We go the wrong way, 
we lose two or three days.

548
00:52:14,550 --> 00:52:17,170
We must follow the gorilla. 
Where she leads.

549
00:52:19,050 --> 00:52:20,640
- That would be left. 
- Best guess...

550
00:52:20,800 --> 00:52:22,810
off my last coordinates was this way.

551
00:52:22,970 --> 00:52:27,890
- That would be right. 
- Amy, Peter, <i>go</i> this way.

552
00:52:29,100 --> 00:52:34,650
Well, I don't have her passion. 
I'll follow the gorilla.

553
00:52:41,910 --> 00:52:44,950
This doesn't seem 
like the right way to me.

554
00:53:11,940 --> 00:53:13,440
Good Lord.

555
00:53:17,320 --> 00:53:21,070
- It's that plane we saw burning in 
the sky. - Who were they?

556
00:53:21,240 --> 00:53:24,870
Travis, my boss, thought I wasn't gonna 
make it. He sent another expedition.

557
00:53:24,990 --> 00:53:29,120
Tried to push too far. Looks like a rocket 
got them. There's no point going in there.

558
00:53:30,920 --> 00:53:35,250
- My God, what a waste of life. 
- We'd better keep moving.

559
00:54:34,980 --> 00:54:38,570
- Oh, shit! Somebody help me. 
- What is it?

560
00:54:38,730 --> 00:54:41,740
There's something on my... 
Like, a creature, growth or something.

561
00:54:41,900 --> 00:54:44,910
- Look. 
- Ah. That's a leech.

562
00:54:45,070 --> 00:54:48,240
- Take it off. 
- It's your leech, you take it off.

563
00:54:48,410 --> 00:54:49,830
- Need some help? 
- No.

564
00:54:49,990 --> 00:54:52,250
- Here, burn it off with this. 
- Ha-ha-ha.

565
00:54:52,410 --> 00:54:53,830
- Ha, ha. 
- Ugh.

566
00:54:53,100 --> 00:54:56,130
- Mm! 
- This is disgusting.

567
00:55:02,760 --> 00:55:04,090
Here.

568
00:55:12,640 --> 00:55:16,230
- Monroe? 
- Don't indicate you see them.

569
00:55:17,270 --> 00:55:22,610
Mizumu. The ghost tribe. 
There are probably 20 of them.

570
00:55:24,450 --> 00:55:27,530
- I only see two. 
- Yes, that's how they are.

571
00:55:27,700 --> 00:55:31,240
- Are they friendly? 
- They're sensitive. They're forest people.

572
00:55:41,630 --> 00:55:45,510
- Why are they laughing? 
- They asked who was in charge, I said I was.

573
00:55:45,670 --> 00:55:47,800
- What's so funny about that? 
- I'm black.

574
00:55:47,970 --> 00:55:50,010
I should have luggage on my head.

575
00:55:58,600 --> 00:56:00,560
He says there's a dead white man...

576
00:56:00,730 --> 00:56:03,480
in this forest with a 
symbol on his clothes.

577
00:56:08,910 --> 00:56:11,700
He's dead? Did they say he's dead?

578
00:56:11,870 --> 00:56:16,710
- What does "TC" mean? 
- TraviCom. The company I work for.

579
00:56:16,870 --> 00:56:19,040
They have several levels of "dead."

580
00:56:19,210 --> 00:56:22,840
Someone's not dead 
until they're completely dead.

581
00:57:02,080 --> 00:57:05,420
They haven't moved him. 
They believe his soul has left his body.

582
00:57:05,630 --> 00:57:09,050
If they move him, his soul will be lost, 
never finding his body again.

583
00:57:09,220 --> 00:57:12,010
They're calling to his soul, 
asking it to come back.

584
00:57:39,410 --> 00:57:40,710
Where is the man?

585
00:57:54,970 --> 00:57:56,260
- It's not him. 
- Not who?

586
00:57:56,430 --> 00:57:58,850
It's Bob Driscoll. What happened to him?

587
00:57:59,020 --> 00:58:01,390
Bob? Bob?

588
00:58:01,520 --> 00:58:03,520
- Let's sit him up. 
- Who is this man?

589
00:58:03,690 --> 00:58:07,820
My company sent an expedition to 
the Virungas. This man was with them.

590
00:58:09,030 --> 00:58:10,280
Lay him over there.

591
00:58:10,440 --> 00:58:13,200
- What happened to him? 
- I don't know.

592
00:58:14,740 --> 00:58:16,240
Bob?

593
00:58:46,650 --> 00:58:48,650
- He's dead. 
- God, no.

594
00:58:57,990 --> 00:59:00,080
That region of the Congo is uninhabited.

595
00:59:00,240 --> 00:59:02,410
- Something inhabits it. 
- What did you see?

596
00:59:02,580 --> 00:59:05,290
Camp destroyed. People dead. 
A gray gorilla or something.

597
00:59:05,460 --> 00:59:07,500
- No such thing as a gray gorilla. 
- I saw one.

598
00:59:07,670 --> 00:59:10,170
- That's hard to believe. 
- Two men are unaccounted for.

599
00:59:10,340 --> 00:59:12,840
Jeffrey Weems 
and Charles Travis, my fiancé.

600
00:59:13,010 --> 00:59:14,510
- My former fiancé. 
- Former?

601
00:59:14,680 --> 00:59:16,130
We'd better get to him, then.

602
00:59:32,030 --> 00:59:34,030
- What the hell is that? 
- Perimeter alarm.

603
00:59:34,190 --> 00:59:36,530
They had it around their camp.

604
00:59:36,910 --> 00:59:39,740
But where's the camp, their equipment?

605
00:59:57,260 --> 01:00:02,010
I don't understand it. There had to be 
300 pounds of equipment.

606
01:00:02,180 --> 01:00:04,890
And the bodies. 
There's no sign of the bodies.

607
01:00:32,960 --> 01:00:35,550
- It's a dead end? 
- Maybe, maybe not.

608
01:00:46,480 --> 01:00:47,810
Yes. Bingo!

609
01:01:40,320 --> 01:01:43,660
- Son of a bitch. 
- Jesus.

610
01:01:43,820 --> 01:01:45,780
What is this?

611
01:01:47,410 --> 01:01:50,370
It is the city of Zinj...

612
01:01:52,580 --> 01:01:56,500
that I have looked for all my life.

613
01:02:02,050 --> 01:02:05,100
See, the eyes.

614
01:02:06,350 --> 01:02:08,520
It is the same.

615
01:02:11,230 --> 01:02:13,850
Kahega, guns up. Stay alert.

616
01:02:19,690 --> 01:02:22,320
Come here, Amy. Stay with me.

617
01:04:11,350 --> 01:04:16,560
First they found the diamonds, 
Solomon's men.

618
01:04:18,270 --> 01:04:21,690
A diamond mine of incredible bounty.

619
01:04:22,940 --> 01:04:29,200
Then they built the city around 
the mine, so that it should be protected.

620
01:04:29,740 --> 01:04:32,660
The savagery of the guards was legend.

621
01:04:32,830 --> 01:04:37,120
They instantly fell upon 
any thief, any transgressor.

622
01:04:37,290 --> 01:04:41,420
The diamonds flowed 
to the kingdom of Solomon.

623
01:04:41,590 --> 01:04:43,710
What happened? Why did the city die?

624
01:04:43,880 --> 01:04:46,130
- I don't know. 
- Maybe the mines ran dry.

625
01:04:46,300 --> 01:04:49,470
No! No, the diamonds are here!

626
01:04:49,630 --> 01:04:52,720
- Monroe, over here! 
- What is it?

627
01:04:56,680 --> 01:04:58,600
On the step, by the hood.

628
01:04:58,770 --> 01:05:00,650
I found this.

629
01:05:07,610 --> 01:05:09,320
Don't go in there. I'll check it out.

630
01:05:09,490 --> 01:05:13,950
- Give me a gun and a flashlight. 
- Don't be so John Wayne, you could get killed.

631
01:05:15,580 --> 01:05:18,750
Now that I've said that, 
I'm not going in there alone, either.

632
01:05:18,910 --> 01:05:21,750
- I must go. 
- Let's all look.

633
01:05:21,880 --> 01:05:23,460
I'll just stay out here with Amy.

634
01:05:23,630 --> 01:05:25,750
Not alone you won't. Kahega!

635
01:05:51,820 --> 01:05:54,620
Stay close together. Homolka...

636
01:06:01,040 --> 01:06:06,040
- Homolka! 
- The same hieroglyphics, over and over.

637
01:06:07,670 --> 01:06:11,220
- What do they mean? 
- Homolka!

638
01:06:16,640 --> 01:06:17,970
Amy, don't wander off!

639
01:06:19,100 --> 01:06:22,100
- So do you speak English? 
- Yes.

640
01:06:22,690 --> 01:06:24,900
- What's your name? 
- Claude.

641
01:06:25,060 --> 01:06:31,400
Claude? Heh. That's an unusual name for 
somebody from, um... Where are you from?

642
01:06:31,570 --> 01:06:36,280
- Mombasa. 
- It's an unusual name for Mombasa.

643
01:06:38,410 --> 01:06:41,960
- Have you ever been to Mombasa? 
- Uh, no.

644
01:06:43,790 --> 01:06:46,420
Then what do you know about it?

645
01:06:51,550 --> 01:06:53,300
I want to go home.

646
01:06:58,220 --> 01:06:59,560
Amy?

647
01:07:02,020 --> 01:07:04,100
Amy, come here.

648
01:07:04,270 --> 01:07:08,480
Where are you, girl? 
Come on, I'm not fooling around.

649
01:07:11,570 --> 01:07:12,820
Amy.

650
01:07:13,900 --> 01:07:15,490
Amy?

651
01:07:18,070 --> 01:07:21,540
Come here, girl. Amy.

652
01:07:33,880 --> 01:07:35,630
Charles!

653
01:07:37,890 --> 01:07:39,550
Charlie!

654
01:07:41,600 --> 01:07:43,810
- What are those holes? 
- Lava tubes.

655
01:07:43,980 --> 01:07:46,850
Molten rock must be flowing 
through this whole place.

656
01:07:47,020 --> 01:07:49,400
This must lead to the mine.

657
01:07:50,980 --> 01:07:54,530
Please, bring a light.

658
01:07:56,650 --> 01:07:58,070
Here.

659
01:08:00,700 --> 01:08:04,200
Now here. Come on.

660
01:08:04,370 --> 01:08:07,710
Up here. No...!

661
01:08:07,870 --> 01:08:11,460
- That's not going anywhere. 
- Where could they be? What happened?

662
01:08:13,760 --> 01:08:16,970
Shit! Let's get out of 
here while we still can.

663
01:08:17,130 --> 01:08:18,130
Help me!

664
01:08:19,050 --> 01:08:21,390
- What's that? 
- Richard!

665
01:08:22,010 --> 01:08:24,970
- Richard? 
- Help me!

666
01:08:27,350 --> 01:08:28,190
- Richard! 
-

667
01:08:28,270 --> 01:08:30,650
No! Richard! Oh, my God! Something...

668
01:09:03,890 --> 01:09:05,350
- Richard. 
- Come on.

669
01:09:05,520 --> 01:09:07,600
Let's get out of here.

670
01:09:11,270 --> 01:09:14,020
- It's okay. 
- Peter.

671
01:09:28,870 --> 01:09:31,370
They're gorillas. 
They're some kind of gorillas.

672
01:09:34,040 --> 01:09:38,800
I never saw an animal move like 
that. They just... Look. They just kill.

673
01:09:38,970 --> 01:09:43,800
That's why the diamonds were never 
found. The myth of the killer ape is true.

674
01:09:43,970 --> 01:09:45,640
I can't believe it.

675
01:10:12,170 --> 01:10:14,170
<i>Bad gorillas.</i>

676
01:10:15,420 --> 01:10:18,460
Let me turn your volume down.

677
01:10:22,300 --> 01:10:24,220
Here's your lovey.

678
01:10:27,310 --> 01:10:29,060
Shh.

679
01:10:29,220 --> 01:10:33,520
<i>- It's</i> okay. 
<i>- Mother. Mother.</i>

680
01:10:33,690 --> 01:10:35,150
Yes, you're okay now.

681
01:10:44,160 --> 01:10:48,030
- See anything? 
- No, but I'm the last line of defense.

682
01:10:48,160 --> 01:10:50,830
They have sensor-operated machine pistols 
on tripods.

683
01:10:50,100 --> 01:10:53,750
Anything comes within 40 ft., 
get ready to the 4th of July.

684
01:10:53,920 --> 01:10:56,130
What can I say, you 
really know how to pack.

685
01:11:07,140 --> 01:11:09,220
- I don't see anything. 
- Put that out.

686
01:11:30,160 --> 01:11:32,080
They're testing their perimeter.

687
01:11:40,340 --> 01:11:41,750
How intelligent are they?

688
01:11:41,920 --> 01:11:44,300
Gorillas are very smart, 
but they're not aggressive.

689
01:11:44,470 --> 01:11:48,010
- These things are very aggressive. 
- I'd say they're smart. Too damn smart.

690
01:11:48,180 --> 01:11:51,140
They're out there, but what are they doing?

691
01:11:51,810 --> 01:11:53,100
Monroe!

692
01:12:51,780 --> 01:12:52,990
Get down!

693
01:13:03,800 --> 01:13:06,800
- They're gone. 
- Couple of hours until first light.

694
01:13:06,920 --> 01:13:08,970
Then we're out of here.

695
01:13:11,390 --> 01:13:16,770
Those hieroglyphics 
we saw inscribed everywhere?

696
01:13:17,270 --> 01:13:19,480
- I have translated them. 
- What do they say?

697
01:13:19,640 --> 01:13:23,690
"We are watching you."

698
01:13:37,290 --> 01:13:40,420
We must have hit some of them. 
Where are the bodies?

699
01:13:40,580 --> 01:13:42,540
- Have you seen Amy? She's gone. 
- No.

700
01:13:42,710 --> 01:13:47,380
She's joined the club, then. We've got two 
porters missing and Homolka's run off, too.

701
01:13:47,550 --> 01:13:51,380
- I'm going to find her. 
- People come first, then gorillas.

702
01:13:51,550 --> 01:13:52,890
- Do you agree? 
- Yes.

703
01:13:53,050 --> 01:13:58,020
Good. We'll retrieve our comrades, 
then we'll retrieve your missing gorilla.

704
01:13:58,180 --> 01:14:01,770
Then we'll get the hell out of here. 
Mount up!

705
01:15:10,460 --> 01:15:11,800
What's that?

706
01:15:35,820 --> 01:15:38,950
Here's the story. 
They domesticated gorillas.

707
01:15:39,120 --> 01:15:41,870
You call that 
domesticated? They're killing a man.

708
01:15:42,040 --> 01:15:46,580
They taught them to be this way. 
They bred them to violence.

709
01:15:46,710 --> 01:15:49,920
They looked for the trait 
and they encouraged it.

710
01:15:50,460 --> 01:15:53,800
Guard dogs, and they 
turned on their masters.

711
01:15:55,680 --> 01:15:57,840
What's that smell?

712
01:15:59,720 --> 01:16:01,510
These are all the bones of gorillas.

713
01:16:01,680 --> 01:16:04,890
- You mean, these are...? 
- No, these are normal gorillas.

714
01:16:07,440 --> 01:16:10,110
- This one's skull was crushed. 
- Feel that?

715
01:16:10,270 --> 01:16:11,440
Feel what?

716
01:16:27,500 --> 01:16:28,960
Hang on!

717
01:16:36,880 --> 01:16:40,260
- Everybody okay? 
- Yeah, let's go.

718
01:16:43,180 --> 01:16:47,060
Don't go this way. It's all caved in. 
God, I'm so glad to see you.

719
01:16:47,180 --> 01:16:51,270
- I was trying to find the mines... 
- Be quiet. Got any flares, Kahega?

720
01:16:51,440 --> 01:16:52,860
Yeah!

721
01:16:55,530 --> 01:16:58,240
- What's making my eyes burn? 
- Sulfur fumes.

722
01:16:58,400 --> 01:17:00,700
Let's hope there's another way out of here.

723
01:17:14,090 --> 01:17:15,500
Careful.

724
01:17:35,730 --> 01:17:40,320
- What the hell...? 
- It's the mines!

725
01:17:40,490 --> 01:17:45,330
"We are watching you." It's the trainers 
talking. The teacher is watching you.

726
01:17:45,830 --> 01:17:47,370
Dead teachers.

727
01:17:52,750 --> 01:17:53,960
Look.

728
01:17:59,090 --> 01:18:03,800
Yes, yes, yes. I knew it was true!

729
01:18:05,220 --> 01:18:09,020
The city of Zinj, the diamonds...

730
01:18:12,650 --> 01:18:17,730
Every word of it was absolutely true.

731
01:18:19,990 --> 01:18:21,740
I'd leave those alone.

732
01:18:28,160 --> 01:18:30,100
- Any other way out of here? 
- Not that I've noticed.

733
01:18:32,790 --> 01:18:35,710
Help me. Fill up everything.

734
01:18:46,350 --> 01:18:49,350
- You've got to get me out of here! 
- Take a number.

735
01:18:49,520 --> 01:18:52,640
- But we're going to be rich! 
- ls that a way out?

736
01:18:52,810 --> 01:18:53,850
Maybe.

737
01:19:28,930 --> 01:19:30,470
Look out!

738
01:19:52,290 --> 01:19:54,620
- Let's go. 
- Stay close together.

739
01:20:01,300 --> 01:20:05,380
- What is it? 
- It's a geode. A giant diamond.

740
01:20:07,090 --> 01:20:10,930
- They cut him off. Kahega! 
- Check if there's a way out back there.

741
01:20:20,400 --> 01:20:21,980
Kahega!

742
01:20:46,720 --> 01:20:48,510
Jeffrey...

743
01:20:50,260 --> 01:20:51,970
Charlie!

744
01:20:53,100 --> 01:20:54,560
Charlie!

745
01:20:55,850 --> 01:20:57,770
- It's him? 
- It's him.

746
01:21:02,860 --> 01:21:04,440
Come on.

747
01:21:18,210 --> 01:21:20,620
- Is there a way out back there? 
- No, nothing.

748
01:21:22,040 --> 01:21:26,130
- We're almost out of rounds. 
- Buy me two minutes.

749
01:21:53,780 --> 01:21:55,030
Let me try.

750
01:21:56,580 --> 01:21:58,080
Aah!

751
01:22:29,490 --> 01:22:31,860
Ugly gorillas. Ugly. Go</i> away.

752
01:22:35,990 --> 01:22:37,990
They don't know what to make of it.

753
01:22:48,750 --> 01:22:52,340
<i>- Amy. 
- Mother. Mother.</i>

754
01:22:57,260 --> 01:23:01,270
- Peter hug <i>Amy.</i> 
- Maternal instinct, long may she reign.

755
01:23:01,430 --> 01:23:04,100
- What are you doing? 
- What am I doing? I'll tell you.

756
01:23:04,230 --> 01:23:06,150
- We're getting out. 
- What about them?

757
01:23:06,310 --> 01:23:09,110
Put them on 
the endangered species list.

758
01:23:16,370 --> 01:23:20,240
Come on, Monroe. Let's go! Move!

759
01:23:20,410 --> 01:23:23,540
- What the hell is that? 
- The latest thing in communication.

760
01:23:23,660 --> 01:23:25,580
- Climb! 
- Up here.

761
01:23:27,580 --> 01:23:30,170
- Go! Go! 
- Come on.

762
01:24:08,380 --> 01:24:09,790
Come on!

763
01:24:16,630 --> 01:24:18,760
Come on, girl. Come on!

764
01:24:29,900 --> 01:24:31,110
GO, Amy!

765
01:24:53,550 --> 01:24:55,420
Keep going!

766
01:24:57,800 --> 01:25:00,510
- Unh. 
- Come on, come on.

767
01:25:04,310 --> 01:25:05,560
Wait!

768
01:25:08,350 --> 01:25:10,060
- Where are they? 
- I don't know.

769
01:25:29,370 --> 01:25:31,000
Hang on!

770
01:25:34,670 --> 01:25:36,550
Watch out!

771
01:25:38,300 --> 01:25:41,090
- Go, Amy! 
- Hurry!

772
01:25:41,260 --> 01:25:44,180
Just hurry! Come on!

773
01:25:52,100 --> 01:25:55,020
- Come on! 
- You can do it!

774
01:25:56,780 --> 01:25:58,070
Keep moving.

775
01:25:59,070 --> 01:26:00,820
Head for higher ground.

776
01:26:01,700 --> 01:26:03,450
Keep going, Amy!

777
01:26:34,150 --> 01:26:37,230
Should be a big yellow case 
with a red stripe on that plane.

778
01:26:37,400 --> 01:26:40,280
It's a balloon. I'll get the transmitter.

779
01:26:42,280 --> 01:26:43,410
Amy!

780
01:26:48,160 --> 01:26:49,830
Amy!

781
01:26:50,910 --> 01:26:53,080
- How close is the border? 
- Not far.

782
01:26:53,250 --> 01:26:55,170
This is Karen Ross, 81452.

783
01:26:55,330 --> 01:27:00,550
That is KR 81452. 
Houston, Travis, do you read?

784
01:27:00,710 --> 01:27:03,800
<i>KR 81452.</i>

785
01:27:03,970 --> 01:27:06,510
Houston, Travis, do you read?

786
01:27:06,720 --> 01:27:13,640
81452, that is KR 81452. 
Houston, Travis, do you read?

787
01:27:15,850 --> 01:27:19,730
- Karen, is that you? 
<i>- It's</i> me, Travis.

788
01:27:19,900 --> 01:27:22,900
I thought I'd lost you. 
I sent another expedition.

789
01:27:23,030 --> 01:27:29,200
<i>- Did</i> you get <i>it?</i> 
- I have bad news. Charles was killed.

790
01:27:29,580 --> 01:27:34,080
Did you get the diamond? Did you?

791
01:27:37,540 --> 01:27:41,670
<i>- Yes.</i> 
- Good girl.

792
01:27:43,880 --> 01:27:49,550
Do you remember when I told you 
that if I ever knew you sent me here...

793
01:27:49,720 --> 01:27:54,520
for some diamond, 
and not for Charlie, I'd make you sorry?

794
01:27:54,980 --> 01:27:57,900
<i>Karen, of course I'm upset.</i>

795
01:27:58,020 --> 01:28:03,150
<i>- But what's done is done. I could...</i> 
- I <i>know. You need</i> a <i>new</i> cash <i>machine.</i>

796
01:28:03,320 --> 01:28:05,030
<i>What would happen to the satellite...</i>

797
01:28:05,200 --> 01:28:08,200
if I put its phone number in the laser 
and pulled the trigger?

798
01:28:08,370 --> 01:28:11,450
The chip from the transmitter's 
got the satellite's number.

799
01:28:15,580 --> 01:28:21,460
It's a 12,000 channel chip. 
If you put it through the diamond... Karen!

800
01:28:26,550 --> 01:28:28,390
This is for you, Charlie.

801
01:28:32,220 --> 01:28:33,640
No!

802
01:28:36,940 --> 01:28:38,230
Amy!

803
01:28:41,230 --> 01:28:42,690
There you are.

804
01:28:51,740 --> 01:28:53,990
Amy, you saved me.

805
01:28:56,580 --> 01:28:58,250
It's okay.

806
01:29:15,930 --> 01:29:17,480
Thank you.

807
01:29:21,560 --> 01:29:23,610
I love you, too.

808
01:29:25,570 --> 01:29:27,110
Mother?

809
01:29:30,570 --> 01:29:33,240
Oh, I see. It's Africa. It's your mother.

810
01:29:35,240 --> 01:29:38,330
Amy, where did you go?

811
01:29:40,210 --> 01:29:43,710
Good gorillas? What good gorillas?

812
01:29:52,180 --> 01:29:53,430
Handsome fella.

813
01:30:19,290 --> 01:30:20,750
Amy...

814
01:30:27,380 --> 01:30:29,920
Look at that. 
ls that Amy with the...?

815
01:30:30,090 --> 01:30:33,760
The silverback. So she's...

816
01:30:35,760 --> 01:30:38,810
I see. I see.

817
01:30:43,020 --> 01:30:44,900
She's home.

818
01:31:02,210 --> 01:31:04,580
- Will she be all right? 
- They know what to do.

819
01:31:04,750 --> 01:31:06,040
It's us I'm worried about.

820
01:31:06,210 --> 01:31:09,800
- We've got to go. Come on. 
- Bye, Amy.

821
01:31:44,920 --> 01:31:47,500
- Peter, would you do me a favor? 
- What?

822
01:31:47,670 --> 01:31:49,590
Would you throw this away for me.

823
01:31:50,380 --> 01:31:52,050
You sure?

824
01:31:55,260 --> 01:31:56,260
Ouch.

825
01:32:02,890 --> 01:32:06,650
- There's a wind. 
- I hope it blows us someplace good.

826
01:32:06,810 --> 01:32:08,440
Me too.
